The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
(guint32*)&RMPP->Type;
(guint32*)&RMPP->Status;
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the code directly casts the addresses of `RMPP->Type` and `RMPP->Status` to `guint32*`, which can result in unaligned memory access on architectures that do not support it. This can lead to undefined behavior, including crashes or incorrect data processing.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
guint32 val;
RMPP_type_item = proto_tree_add_item_ret_uint(RMPP_header_tree, hf_opa_rmpp_type, tvb, local_offset, 1, ENC_BIG_ENDIAN, &val);
RMPP->Type = val;
```
Reason:
The fix involves using a temporary variable `val` to store the value retrieved from the `tvb` instead of directly writing to `RMPP->Type` and `RMPP->Status`. This avoids unaligned access by ensuring that the data is first read into a properly aligned variable before being assigned to the structure members.

